oing with a single main.go file using only the standard library, following the source header convention, and leveraging bufio.Scanner for line-by-line processing with io.Copy as a fallback for raw passthrough when no flags are set. That keeps it simple and efficient.\n\nBefore I write the final YAML, I should figure out the correct PRD file path—looking at the existing ones like prd001-testutils.yaml and prd002-sys.yaml, I need to find where the cat PRD lives in the docs/specs/product-requirements directory, but I don't have the exact filename yet.\n\nWriting the YAML spec... lines 1-4 for source file header convention)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/cat/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Complete GNU cat implementation: file/stdin concatenation, -n/-b line numbering, -s blank line squeezing, -v/-E/-T/-A/-e/-t character display, exit codes\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Read files named on the command line in argument order and write their contents to stdout; when no file arguments are given, or when '-' appears as an argument, read from stdin at that position in the sequence\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement line decoration flags: -n numbers all output lines starting from 1; -b numbers only non-blank output lines starting from 1 and overrides -n when both are specified; -s squeezes multiple adjacent blank lines into a single blank line\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement character display flags: -v shows non-printing characters using ^X and M-X notation; -E appends '$' at the end of each line; -T displays tabs as ^I; -A is equivalent to -vET; -e is equivalent to -vE; -t is equivalent to -vT\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Exit with code 0 when all files are processed successfully; exit with code 1 when any file cannot be opened or read; print each per-file error to stderr and continue processing remaining arguments\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Place all code in a single cmd/cat/main.go per the architecture convention: 'Each package contains main.go (implementation)'\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use Go standard library only -- flag for CLI flag parsing, bufio for line-oriented processing, io for raw byte passthrough, os for file and stream I/O\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Follow the project source header convention: copyright line '// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.', SPDX license identifier, then package doc comment referencing the PRD\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"When no line-processing flags (-n, -b, -s, -v, -E, -T) are active, use io.Copy for zero-allocation byte passthrough; when any line-processing flag is active, use bufio.Scanner for line-at-a-time processing\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/cat/main.go compiles without errors and runs successfully with no external dependencies beyond Go standard library\"\n\nI'm mapping out the design approach: I'll stick to Go's standard library with flag parsing and bufio for line processing, but switch to direct byte copying when no transformations are needed. The source file needs the proper copyright header and license identifier, and I'll handle the various flag combinations to control line numbering, blank line squeezing, and character display options.\n\nThe acceptance criteria cover the core functionality: building the binary, reading from files or stdin, implementing line numbering with the -n and -b flags where -b takes precedence, collapsing consecutive blank lines with -s, and ensuring the program continues processing remaining files even when one fails to open.\n\nI'm reconsidering the requirement structure—I have 4 requirements but the guidelines suggest 5-8, and I'm trying to figure out if I should break down the character display options into their own requirement or keep them grouped. Let me reorganize: file input handling, stdin input, line numbering, blank line squeezing, character display modes, and error handling. That gives me 6 requirements, which fits better, though now I need to make sure the PRD sub-requirements don't exceed 4. -b line numbering (with -b overriding -n), R4: -s blank line squeezing, R5: -v/-E/-T/-A/-e/-t character display, and R6: exit codes and error handling. That gives me 6 requirements covering 4 PRD sub-requirement areas with 7 acceptance criteria and 4 design decisions, which all fit within the constraints.\n\nNow I'm estimating the line count to make sure this task will produce something in the 150-250 range. Estimating the Go implementation...\n\nEstimating line counts for the implementation...\n\nI'm realizing the cmd/cat/ directory doesn't exist yet since the architecture shows cmd/ grows with the roadmap, so I'll need to create main.go from scratch. I should also consider whether to include the -u flag for unbuffered output, though it's typically a no-op in modern systems.\n\nFor the issue title, I need to follow the pattern of existing issues and include requirement references. Something like \"prd-cat R1-R4: cmd/cat implementation -- file concatenation, line numbering, character display, exit codes\" would capture the scope across the four main requirement areas.\n\nActually, I should map this to the actual use case instead. The cat functionality is part of rel01.1-uc001-cat, so the title should reflect that directly: \"rel01.1-uc001-cat: cmd/cat implementation -- input handling, line decoration, character display, exit codes\". This clearly ties the implementation task to the roadmap and avoids any overlap with the existing #232 issue about pkg/sys tests.\n\nNow I'm laying out the required reading and file structure for the implementation, starting with the cat PRD, the use case specification, test suite definitions, and the architectural conventions I need to follow.\n\nLooking at the specific requirements: I need to handle file concatenation with stdin fallback, implement line numbering with the -n and -b flags where -b takes precedence, and format line numbers as right-justified 6-character fields followed by a tab.\n\nFor the display features, I'm implementing blank line squeezing with -s, character display modes with -v, -E, -T flags and their combinations like -A for all three, and proper error handling that exits with code 1 if any file fails while continuing to process remaining arguments.\n\nThe design calls for keeping everything in a single main.go file following the project structure.  I'll use only Go's standard library—flag for argument parsing, bufio for line-by-line processing, io for efficient raw copying when no flags are active, and os for file operations. I need to match the source header format from existing files with copyright, SPDX license, and a package doc comment referencing the cat PRD. The key optimization is detecting whether any line-processing flags are enabled: if none are active, I'll use io.Copy for fast passthrough; otherwise I'll process line by line with bufio to handle the various display transformations.\n\nNow I'm defining the acceptance criteria: the binary must compile cleanly, handle file arguments in order, read from stdin when no files are given, support line numbering with -n and -b flags (where -b overrides -n and only counts non-blank lines), collapse consecutive blank lines with -s, and gracefully handle file open errors by printing to stderr while continuing with remaining files and exiting with code 1. OK wait, 6 requirements might map to more than 4 PRD sub-requirements. Let me reconsider.\n\nIf I view my R1+R2 as mapping to 1 PRD input-handling requirement, R3+R4 as mapping to 1 PRD line-decoration requirement, R5 as mapping to 1 PRD character-display requirement, and R6 as mapping to 1 PRD error-handling requirement, that's 4 PRD sub-requirements.\n\nBut the constraint says \"sub-requirements (e.g., R1.1, R2.3), not requirement groups.\" So it's really about the PRD's sub-requirements, not groups. If the cat PRD has R1.1 (read fi