eptance criteria that need to be included.\n\nActually, I should reconsider the requirement count—splitting input handling into separate file and stdin requirements makes sense since they're distinct behaviors, but I need to make sure that aligns with what's actually in the PRD rather than adding my own breakdown.\n\nLet me verify the constraints again: the limit is 4 PRD sub-requirements per task, and code tasks should target 5-8 requirements total with 5-8 acceptance criteria and 3-5 design decisions. I need to make sure I'm not inflating the count by splitting a single requirement into multiple sub-bullets, and that each requirement maps cleanly to the task without merging unrelated things together.\n\nWait, I'm seeing a potential contradiction here. If I need 5-8 task requirements but can only have 4 PRD sub-requirements, and each PRD requirement maps to exactly one task requirement, then I'd need 5-8 PRD requirements to support that many task requirements. But the constraint says at most 4 PRD sub-requirements. This only resolves if \"requirements\" and \"sub-requirements\" are different levels of the hierarchy.\n\nSo the constraint is specifically on the granular sub-requirements like R1.1, R2.3—I can touch at most 4 of those. But I can have 5-8 task requirements that collectively map to and elaborate on those 4 sub-requirements, with multiple task requirements potentially addressing different aspects of the same sub-requirement. I'm reconsidering whether the cat task is actually the right starting point. Looking at the releases with complete specs, rel00.0 has source code already implemented, but rel01.1 for cat is the first one that needs code written. Though I should double-check if there's anything from rel00.0 that's still missing—like the cmd/version source code that might need to be created despite the magefiles exclusion rule.\n\nSince I'm not entirely sure about cmd/version's requirements and I have clearer visibility into what cat needs, I'll move forward with cat as the first task. The main deliverables for rel00.0 are the pkg/ packages, which are already implemented, so rel01.1 is the logical next step.\n\nNow I need to make sure the output formatting is correct—YAML literal block scalar for the description, ASCII dashes instead of Unicode, and mappings with id and text fields.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":45783,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":45783},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d725995-2239-4999-be77-a1d2eecd5013","uuid":"c8ca91c7-da18-4bb8-8728-45ee1b81c175"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JwTd9zZEKNKZjRNbJ9mXjE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Key components: `docs/` (specs), `cmd/` (utility implementations), `pkg/testutils/` (differential test harness), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets, externally scaffolded).\n\n**Implementation State**: Source code exists for all three `pkg/` packages — `testutils`, `sys`, `format` — with full test suites. No `cmd/` implementations exist on this branch. Issue #232 (prd002-sys R2.1-R2.3 tests) is in progress but the source code already contains those tests.\n\n**Current Release Priority**: rel00.0 (foundational infrastructure) is effectively complete — source code confirms all pkg/ packages are implemented. The earliest release needing code is **rel01.1** (cat), which has spec_complete status but no code.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\n- rel00.0: pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format all have source code with tests. Magefiles are externally scaffolded (DD7). Effectively complete.\n- **rel01.1 (cat)**: spec_complete, code not started. This is the next actionable target.\n- rel01.2 (sponge), rel01.3 (du), rel03.0 (wc): also spec_complete but depend on having a working cmd/ pattern established by cat first.\n- rel02.0, 02.1, 02.2: specs pending — cannot propose code tasks.\n\nCat is the natural first `cmd/` implementation: it establishes the pattern for all subsequent utilities, is moderately complex, and its specs are complete.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"rel01.1-uc001-cat: cmd/cat implementation -- input handling, line decoration, character display, exit codes\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/ (find and read the cat PRD -- the YAML file whose name contains 'cat'; read all requirements and acceptance criteria)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel01.1-uc001-cat.yaml (end-to-end execution path for cat)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-01.1.yaml (differential test inputs and expected outputs)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (cmd/ component -- one package per utility, main.go for implementation, utility_test.go for differential tests)\n      - pkg/format/color.go (lines 1-4 for project source file header convention -- copyright, SPDX, package doc)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (lines 1-8 for package doc comment pattern referencing PRD)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/cat/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"GNU cat implementation -- file/stdin concatenation, line numbering, blank line squeezing, character display flags, exit codes\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Read files named on the command line in argument order and write their byte-for-byte contents to stdout; when no file arguments are given, or when '-' appears as a file argument, read from stdin at that position in the argument sequence\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement line decoration flags: -n numbers all output lines starting from 1 with a right-justified 6-character field followed by a tab; -b numbers only non-blank output lines starting from 1 and overrides -n when both are specified; -s squeezes multiple consecutive blank lines into a single blank line\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement character display flags: -v shows non-printing characters using ^X notation for control characters and M-X notation for high-bit characters; -E appends '$' at the end of each output line; -T displays tab characters as ^I; -A is equivalent to -vET; -e is equivalent to -vE; -t is equivalent to -vT\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Exit with code 0 when all files are processed successfully; exit with code 1 when any file cannot be opened or read; print per-file error messages to stderr and continue processing remaining file arguments\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Place all code in a single cmd/cat/main.go per the architecture convention: 'Each package contains main.go (implementation) and <utility>_test.go (differential tests)'\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use Go standard library only -- flag for CLI parsing, bufio for line-oriented processing, io for raw byte passthrough, os for file and stream I/O; no external dependencies\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Follow project source header convention from existing pkg/ files: '// Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.' then '// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT' then package doc comment referencing the cat PRD by number and requirement range\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"When no line-processing flags (-n, -b, -s, -v, -E, -T) are active, use io.Copy for efficient zero-allocation byte passthrough; when any flag requiring line inspection is active, process input line by line via bufio.Reader or bufio.Scanner\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/cat/main.go compiles with 'go build ./cmd/cat/' producing a binary with no errors\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Running the binary with one or more file path arguments concatenates their contents to stdout in argument order\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Running the binary with no arguments reads stdin until EOF and writes to stdout\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"The -n flag numbers all output lines in GNU cat format: right-justified 6-character field width followed by a tab separator\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"The -b flag numbers only non-blank output lines and overrides -n when both flags are given\"\n      - id: AC6